+# RGP-PB-08: Publish and recover the selected models
+
+These tools publish the frozen MIL and Hopfield model graphs through VIPER and recover the saved models in a fresh workspace. They include the prepared model inputs and separately recorded earlier builds and raw priors.
+
+## Commands
+
+[prepare.py](prepare.py) reads the frozen inventory and upstream-run report. It checks local source bytes, saves their original locations, and produces a fixed `CopyRequest`. The captured local files are limited to one GiB. Existing GCS files retain their full source addresses. The source workspace needs its original GCS configuration.
+
+[publish.py](publish.py) calls `execution.promote_run_to_cloud()`. It requires write access to the destination named in the request, which may be public or private. `--pilot 1` selects the first Hopfield root for the pilot. The complete request includes all four model roots and supporting source records. The worker keeps its verified progress across restarts. The pilot and full request have separate progress files because their selected scope differs.
+
+[checks.py](checks.py) selects the destination checkpoint, inputs and saved TUNE predictions from the verified snapshot mappings. Existing recovery requests retain their exact bytes when their settings match and every saved file address remains in the completed mappings; a mismatch stops preparation. The comparison rule is exact equality. The saved Python, PyTorch, NumPy, CUDA and GPU versions are required before prediction. The reader accepts MIL's structured execution context and Hopfield's flat runtime record; both become the same five version requirements. Its `register` command writes the additional publication set after all four recovery reports pass.
+
+[recover.py](recover.py) downloads through VIPER's normal reader. Its reader checks the destination repository before every file read. It invokes the existing RICO prediction command, saves the prediction, then compares it with the frozen expected arrays. Repairing an expected-result report reuses the saved prediction. Changed model inputs require a fresh recovery workspace.
+
+The original model records and default storage settings remain unchanged.
+
+## Execution environment
+
+Use the original L4 model environment and VIPER source at commit `462b2029c5ec6797585840fdf8bfd7c9be886b31` through `PYTHONPATH`. The original scientific package environment stays installed. Hugging Face authentication must be available to the machine running the copy. Keep credentials in the normal Hugging Face login store.
+
+The public copy stopped on October 17 after Hugging Face rejected its next commit with HTTP 429 and reported a limit of 128 commits per hour. VIPER now waits after an explicit quota rejection and retries up to three times. It honors a numeric `Retry-After` header, using 3,601 seconds when that header is absent or unrecognized. Other failures return to the existing journal recovery. The saved public request and journal remain the continuation point.
+
+The prepared request on the source VM is:
+
+```text
+/mnt/rico-source-data/viper-publication-20261017/public-request.json
+```
+
+From the RICO checkout root, the copy commands are:
+
+```bash
+publication_root=/mnt/rico-source-data/viper-publication-20261017
+python tools/graph_publication/publish.py "$publication_root/public-request.json" "$publication_root/pilot-result.json" --pilot 1
+python tools/graph_publication/publish.py "$publication_root/public-request.json" "$publication_root/full-result.json"
+```
+
+Run the pilot recovery before starting the full copy. Prepare each recovery request from its copy's `progress.json` and result:
+
+```bash
+python tools/graph_publication/checks.py prepare WORKSPACE PROGRESS_JSON RESULT_JSON CHECK_DIRECTORY
+python tools/graph_publication/recover.py CHECK_JSON FRESH_RECOVERY_DIRECTORY
+python tools/graph_publication/checks.py register "$publication_root/public-request.json" "$publication_root/full-result.json" CHECK_DIRECTORY "$publication_root/additional-publication.json"
+```
+
+When the transfer workspace contains historical model pointers, pass the frozen model metadata directory to `checks.py prepare` as `WORKSPACE`, and pass the same directory to `checks.py register` with `--model-root`. Registration checks the pointer hashes saved in the recovery requests. Keep the transfer request unchanged so its existing journal remains valid.
+
+Run recovery in a new process with fresh VIPER and Hugging Face caches. Mount the reviewed code, numerical environment, credentials for the additional host, and recovery directory. Keep the original model stores outside the recovery process's filesystem. The destination reader rejects original storage addresses. Retain the process command and environment alongside the reports.
+
+## Current evidence
+
+Preparation on the source VM accounted for all 1,305 required payloads, totaling 41,504,155,543 bytes. It found 811 snapshots and 2,674 file references using four model roots and 289 supporting records. The original inventory file's SHA-256 is `0e641ded7af12ac4219e158bd5402bff54785bf1df2e07cd0249c4681c12097e`.
+
+The [public dataset](https://huggingface.co/datasets/pvd232/viper-sota-publication) contains all 1,305 required payloads across 783 completed snapshot mappings; 28 pinned Git source snapshots remain declared external code references. On October 18, independent comparison passed for 2,605 file references and 889 protocol documents, followed by ordinary destination verification for all four model roots. The [registered publication](https://huggingface.co/datasets/pvd232/viper-sota-publication/blob/main/publication/additional-publication.json) records the complete result and four exact TUNE recoveries: MIL seed 123464 and Hopfield seeds 123457, 123458, and 123459. Every maximum absolute error is zero, with absolute and relative tolerances both zero.
+
+Recovery used Python 3.13.15, PyTorch 2.14.0+cu130, NumPy 2.5.3, CUDA 13.0, and an NVIDIA L4. Original model stores and cloud credentials were absent from the recovery environment, and the destination reader rejected other storage addresses. Final registration reused the saved predictions after checking their public addresses against the complete graph. Prediction hashes, sizes, and modification times remained unchanged, as did the original model pointers and source storage settings. The [dataset instructions and checksums](https://huggingface.co/datasets/pvd232/viper-sota-publication) link the fixed requests, journal, comparison, recovery reports, and saved predictions.
+
+Twenty-two controlled tool tests pass. They check stable requests as the journal grows, rejection of changed settings or file references, destination-only reads, prediction reuse during report repair, both committed runtime formats, environment changes, captured source identities, complete four-model registration, and separate frozen metadata with pointer-change rejection. These tests exercise the recovery commands with a small numerical result. The live L4 checks establish the real-model result.
+
+Repository-wide active Ruff and Pyrefly scans both passed across 294 files. Separate archive scans reported 629 Ruff findings and 530 Pyrefly findings across 49 unchanged files. The full lists and outputs are retained with the local migration evidence. The [publication workflow](../../.github/workflows/graph-publication.yml) checks every active Python file and runs the [publication-tool tests](../../tests/test_graph_publication.py).
+
+Independent human review remains pending.
